# netsurf: WebAssembly -- wasm3 behind the WebAssembly API, long calls
# sliced so the screen keeps drawing (patch 254)
# Generated 2026-10-12
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 253)
#
# There was no WebAssembly: QuickJS has none. hCaptcha's proof of work
# (claude.ai's email sign-in) is a WebAssembly module, and so is whatever a
# site builds with wasm-bindgen or emscripten. docs/wasm-hcaptcha-
# measurement.md measured the shape: an MVP interpreter runs it; about a
# minute on the Vita; the UI must not freeze for it.
#
#  - wasm3 0.5.0 (tools/fetch-wasm3.sh, pinned by sha256; patches/wasm3/)
#    built beside libquickjs by tools/build-quickjs.sh (lib/libm3.a,
#    include/wasm3/), -O3 -fwrapv (wasm3 relies on signed overflow
#    wrapping; UBSan caught it), linked by frontends/vita/Makefile.
#  - quickjs/macsurf_wasm.c (new): the natives on the hidden global
#    __wasm. A module's imports and exports come from its own sections;
#    an instance is its own wasm3 environment and runtime with a copy of
#    the bytes; function imports are one trampoline that converts the
#    arguments and calls the JavaScript function; exported functions are
#    native functions (no JavaScript wrapper: wasm-bindgen glue crosses
#    the boundary hundreds of thousands of times). Memory is an
#    ArrayBuffer over wasm3's own bytes, detached when they move (growth
#    reallocates) -- checked whenever control leaves WebAssembly -- which
#    is what wasm-bindgen's "byteLength === 0" cache check expects. A
#    WebAssembly.Memory made by a script is a runtime with no module yet,
#    so views made before instantiating stay valid (emscripten). Traps
#    throw WebAssembly.RuntimeError; an import's exception is carried
#    through the trap and rethrown as the same value. Re-entrant calls
#    (an import calling an export) are wasm3's own.
#  - Slicing: wasm3 called m3_Yield on every wasm call and never in a
#    loop; patches/wasm3/01 gates it by a countdown in both. Every 200 ms
#    of a call the frontend draws the page as it is with "WebAssembly
#    running: N s (O stops)" (macsurf_wasm_slice, vita/main.c); CIRCLE
#    stops it with a RuntimeError, as does a 300 s cap. No JavaScript, no
#    timers and no input reach the page meanwhile: the script is still in
#    the call. (The simulator reads no pad there: its pad drives the
#    scripted autotest.)
#  - The runaway-script deadline runs on the script's own clock now (the
#    real one minus the time spent inside WebAssembly, macsurf_qjs.c), so
#    a minute of proof of work does not end the script that started it;
#    JavaScript run by imports counts as before.
#  - vita_polyfill.js: WebAssembly -- Module (imports / exports /
#    customSections), Instance, Memory, Table (an instance's funcref
#    table: get / length), Global, CompileError / LinkError /
#    RuntimeError, validate, compile, instantiate, compileStreaming /
#    instantiateStreaming (application/wasm, as Chromium requires).
#
# Not done: imported globals and tables, Table.set / grow on an
# instance's table, shared memory, more than one memory or table,
# validating function bodies at compile time (wasm3 compiles a function
# when it is first called; a whole-module compile is seconds on the
# Vita), and running WebAssembly off the main thread.
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-wasm.html, WASM254 15/15 (Chromium 15/15),
# including two ~5 s calls in one script (past its 8 s budget) during
# which the frontend draws a frame every 200 ms; tools/test-wasm.sh runs
# the glue and wasm3 under ASan + UBSan (lifetimes through collections,
# a call stopped from a slice, 3000 mutated modules).
